{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content \\\"D:\\\\Claude\\\\docs\\\\plans\\\\nemesis\\\\NEMESIS-FINAL-IMPLEMENTATION-DISPATCH(3).md\\\" | Select-Object -First 500\"","aggregated_output":"# Nemesis Final Implementation Dispatch — Product-Aware Whole-System Assurance\r\n\r\n**Target:** Orthic Labs Nemesis\r\n**Execution style:** mechanical, specification-led implementation\r\n**Authority:** this dispatch is the sole implementation queue for `NEMESIS-FINAL-IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md`; do not execute superseded plans, addenda, patches, or roadmaps in parallel\r\n**Testing rule:** do not run verification during ordinary tasks. Run only the Book Gate at the end of each book and the final release gate at the end of Book 8.\r\n\r\n## 0. Execution contract\r\n\r\n1. Execute books in numeric order.\r\n2. Execute tasks marked **SERIAL** in order.\r\n3. Tasks sharing the same parallel wave may run concurrently only when their owned paths are disjoint.\r\n4. Integrate a parallel wave only in its named serial integration task.\r\n5. Read every owned file completely before modifying it.\r\n6. Do not alter paths outside a task’s **Own** list.\r\n7. Do not add a second core, second planner, second report truth, second repository inventory, or second skill loader.\r\n8. Do not call external research or select substitute dependencies.\r\n9. Do not run package-manager, verification, benchmark, or test commands before the Book Gate.\r\n10. Do not create repository-host-specific automation files.\r\n11. Do not add version-control procedures to implementation tasks.\r\n12. When a required input is absent, implement the typed missing or unproven state specified here; do not invent data.\r\n13. Every generated artifact must carry producer, schema version, binding, denominator, media type, and digest.\r\n14. Every selected provider must emit one terminal receipt.\r\n15. Every task ends by returning the exact paths changed and any unresolved blocker. Do not execute the next task automatically when a blocker exists.\r\n\r\n### 0.1 Mode legend\r\n\r\n- **SERIAL** — must complete before dependent work starts.\r\n- **P-A / P-B / P-C / P-D** — parallel lane in the current book.\r\n- **INTEGRATE** — serial convergence after all named lanes.\r\n- **GATE** — the only task in the book that runs verification.\r\n\r\n### 0.2 Implementation agent topology\r\n\r\nThis subsection governs implementation of Nemesis only. It does not require an audit run itself to be serial or to use one agent per provider; runtime scheduling is specified in §0.3 and reasoning execution in §0.4.\r\n\r\n1. **One numbered task equals one isolated agent invocation.** The same model or agent implementation may execute many tasks serially, but each task starts with a fresh context containing only this execution contract, the selected task, its dependencies' receipts, and the named normative snippets.\r\n2. A task agent must not spawn child agents, nested subagents, councils, or autonomous background workers.\r\n3. **SERIAL**, **INTEGRATE**, and **GATE** tasks are each executed by one agent invocation.\r\n4. Tasks in **P-A / P-B / P-C / P-D** may execute concurrently as separate agent invocations only after all declared dependencies are complete and their owned paths are disjoint.\r\n5. The outer orchestrator—not a task agent—decides whether eligible parallel tasks run concurrently or serially. Parallel execution is an optimization; executing every task serially remains valid.\r\n6. Parallel workers do not share conversational memory or mutable in-process state. They communicate only through repository files, immutable artifacts, and the standard task result.\r\n7. A task agent stops after returning its standard task result. It must not start another task, merge another worker's output, or widen its ownership.\r\n8. Only the named **INTEGRATE** task may converge the outputs of a completed parallel wave. It must read every prerequisite task receipt before changing integration-owned files.\r\n9. A **BLOCKED** result prevents dependent tasks from starting. It does not authorize a worker to redesign the dependency graph or absorb the blocked work into another task.\r\n10. Agent identity is provenance, not authority. The task contract, owned paths, frozen snippets, and dependency receipts define authority.\r\n\r\n### 0.3 Audit runtime scheduler contract\r\n\r\n1. One deterministic Node ESM coordinator owns the control plane. Repository binding, Cortex projection, product-target discovery, component/stack/external-system graph construction, release-contract resolution, assurance-control and scenario compilation, family/lens/provider selection, denominator construction, dependency validation, execution-policy resolution, and plan sealing are strictly serial.\r\n2. After sealing, provider execution is a resource-aware DAG. The host may execute ready providers serially or concurrently; it may never run a provider before every declared dependency and consumed artifact is terminal and valid.\r\n3. Every provider declares `scheduleClass` as `serial`, `parallel-safe`, `parallel-bounded`, or `exclusive`. `serial` runs alone. `parallel-safe` runs whenever dependencies and resources permit. `parallel-bounded` also respects its group ceiling. `exclusive` acquires every named concurrency key before starting.\r\n4. Every provider declares resource claims for CPU units, memory MiB, I/O units, project-execution slots, browser slots, native-surface slots, virtual-machine slots, simulator/emulator slots, physical-device slots, external-system slots, reviewer slots, and signing slots. The sealed profile supplies pool ceilings. Safe defaults are provider concurrency 4, project execution 1, browser 1, native surface 1, virtual machine 1, simulator/emulator 1, physical device 1, external system 2, reviewer 2, signing 1; explicit host or user values may lower or raise ceilings before sealing.\r\n5. Project/build providers sharing a component root, build directory, package-manager cache, generated-output path, database fixture, emulator/device, browser profile, native window server, or signing identity use the same sealed `concurrencyKey` and cannot overlap when the contract marks that key exclusive.\r\n6. `--schedule serial` forces one admitted provider at a time. `--schedule auto` uses every available parallel opportunity within sealed ceilings. `--concurrency` and `--review-concurrency` set ceilings before plan sealing. A host may use fewer workers than the ceilings but never more.\r\n7. The plan writes `execution-plan.json` containing nodes, dependencies, potential waves, barriers, schedule classes, resource claims, concurrency keys, failure policies, reasoning requirements, and cache keys. The run writes `schedule-receipt.json` containing actual admission, start, completion, blocking, cancellation, retry, cache, and checkpoint events.\r\n8. Parallel completion order is non-semantic. Provider outputs, findings, candidates, variants, and final reports are canonically ordered. Serial and auto execution of the same sealed fixture must produce the same semantic verification digest.\r\n9. Provider failure blocks dependents with typed receipts but does not stop independent nodes unless the provider declares `failurePolicy: abort-run` or an integrity failure invalidates the plan. The scheduler never adds fallback providers or changes a denominator after observing a result.\r\n10. Cancellation stops admission of new work, interrupts only providers whose contracts allow interruption, flushes completed artifacts, records incomplete/blocked nodes, and writes a resumable checkpoint. Resume requires the same binding, plan digest, provider versions, execution policy, and valid cached artifact inputs.\r\n11. Content-addressed reuse is permitted only when provider version, rules, denominator, input artifact digests, repository binding, host capability class, semantic environment, and cache policy match. Cache reuse is an explicit receipt, not an implied pass.\r\n12. Reconciliation, policy evaluation, finalization, and final semantic projection generation are serial barriers. Independent out-of-band verification may replay its own provider DAG under the same schedule contract.\r\n\r\n### 0.4 Audit reasoning and judgment contract\r\n\r\n1. Every lens, rule, and provider declares `reasoningRequirement` as `none`, `bounded-review`, `independent-adjudication`, or `human-decision`.\r\n2. Every reasoning contract declares `reviewTrigger` as `never`, `candidate`, `applicable-subject`, or `policy`, plus one subject kind such as candidate, attack path, UX flow, rendered surface, copy item, narrative unit, AI-evaluation case, safety hazard, or remediation proposal.\r\n3. Deterministic and measured providers use `reasoningRequirement: none`; they must not call a model or use an agent to decide an objective result.\r\n4. Nemesis uses progressive narrowing: inventory and cheap deterministic checks first; measured runtime evidence second; bounded review only for applicable holistic subjects or surviving candidates; independent adjudication only where the lifecycle requires it; semantic variants only for confirmed root causes; synthesis last.\r\n5. Reasoning subjects may fan out concurrently as separate fresh reviewer contexts up to the sealed reviewer pool. Reviewers receive immutable packets and do not share conversational memory or mutable state.\r\n6. A reviewer may be implemented by the host as an isolated subagent, local model, remote model, human review system, or another qualified mechanism. Nemesis depends on the packet, isolation, identity, and receipt contract rather than vendor terminology.\r\n7. A reviewer receives only declared evidence and artifact references. It cannot browse arbitrary repository content, execute tools, mutate files, alter the plan, change a denominator, change a schema, add work, or write directly to final reports.\r\n8. A producer cannot adjudicate or verify its own output. Context reuse, producer/reviewer identity collision, invalid schema, missing counterargument, insufficient evidence, truncation, or unqualified reviewer identity yields `UNPROVEN` or `BLOCKED`.\r\n9. `--reasoning disabled` invokes no reviewers and leaves every applicable reasoning-required subject unproven. `--reasoning auto` invokes eligible reviewers when the host capability exists. `--reasoning required` blocks plan completion when a required qualified reviewer is unavailable. The selected mode and reviewer budgets are sealed.\r\n10. Full and release profiles may require one holistic review for each declared UX surface, visual surface, copy scope, narrative sequence, AI-evaluation scope, or safety-hazard scope even when no deterministic candidate exists. Such always-applicable review is declared by `reviewTrigger: applicable-subject`, never inferred at runtime.\r\n11. Reviewer output must validate against the sealed schema and binding before becoming an immutable judgment receipt. Disagreement is preserved as separate receipts and resolved only through configured policy.\r\n12. All joins, variant eligibility, policy decisions, report generation, and semantic verification after reviewer receipts are deterministic.\r\n\r\n### 0.5 Standard task result\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTASK: B#-###\r\nSTATUS: COMPLETE | BLOCKED\r\nCHANGED:\r\n  path -> purpose\r\nARTIFACTS:\r\n  path -> digest or not-generated\r\nDEVIATIONS:\r\n  none | exact path mapping forced by existing code\r\nBLOCKER:\r\n  none | exact missing input and the file/function that requires it\r\nNEXT:\r\n  next task ID only\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 1. Normative implementation snippets\r\n\r\nThese snippets define contracts. Copy them exactly except for repository-relative import paths that already exist.\r\n\r\n### SNIP-01 — provider, evidence, and judgment enums\r\n\r\n```js\r\nexport const PROVIDER_STATUS = Object.freeze([\r\n  'pass', 'fail', 'partial', 'unproven', 'skipped',\r\n  'error', 'pending', 'missing', 'candidates', 'blocked',\r\n]);\r\n\r\nexport const PROVIDER_ROLE = Object.freeze([\r\n  'deterministic', 'model-builder', 'candidate-generator',\r\n  'hypothesis-generator', 'scenario-runner', 'external-evidence-importer',\r\n  'adjudicator', 'variant-analyzer', 'evidence-synthesizer',\r\n  'renderer', 'verifier',\r\n]);\r\n\r\nexport const EVIDENCE_CLASS = Object.freeze([\r\n  'observed', 'measured', 'derived', 'inferred', 'interpretive',\r\n]);\r\n\r\nexport const JUDGMENT_VERDICT = Object.freeze([\r\n  'CONFIRMED', 'LIKELY', 'REVIEW_REQUIRED',\r\n  'REJECTED', 'NOT_APPLICABLE', 'UNPROVEN', 'BLOCKED',\r\n]);\r\n\r\nexport const SCHEDULE_CLASS = Object.freeze([\r\n  'serial', 'parallel-safe', 'parallel-bounded', 'exclusive',\r\n]);\r\n\r\nexport const REASONING_REQUIREMENT = Object.freeze([\r\n  'none', 'bounded-review', 'independent-adjudication', 'human-decision',\r\n]);\r\n\r\nexport const REVIEW_TRIGGER = Object.freeze([\r\n  'never', 'candidate', 'applicable-subject', 'policy',\r\n]);\r\n```\r\n\r\n### SNIP-02 — audit family record\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\r\n  \"id\": \"copy\",\r\n  \"title\": \"Copy assurance\",\r\n  \"selector\": { \"op\": \"hasUserVisibleContent\" },\r\n  \"dependsOn\": [\"integrity\", \"content.inventory\"],\r\n  \"providers\": [],\r\n  \"denominatorKind\": \"user-visible-content-items\",\r\n  \"evidenceClasses\": [\"observed\", \"derived\", \"interpretive\"],\r\n  \"requiredForComprehensiveClaim\": true,\r\n  \"benchmark\": {\r\n    \"status\": \"unproven\",\r\n    \"providerVersion\": null,\r\n    \"corpusDigest\": null,\r\n    \"qualificationDigest\": null\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### SNIP-03 — core API and host\r\n\r\n```js\r\nexport async function inspectProduct(options, host) {}\r\nexport async function buildPlan(options, host) {}\r\nexport async function executePlan(plan, host) {}\r\nexport function reconcileRun({ plan, receipts, artifacts }, host) {}\r\nexport async function adjudicateRun({ plan, facts, candidates }, host) {}\r\nexport function finalizeRun({ plan, facts, adjudication, policy }, host) {}\r\nexport async function verifyRun({ priorRun, currentRepository }, host) {}\r\n\r\nexport function createHost(overrides = {}) {\r\n  return {\r\n    clock: overrides.clock,\r\n    random: overrides.random,\r\n    fs: overrides.fs,\r\n    processRunner: overrides.processRunner,\r\n    browser: overrides.browser ?? null,\r\n    reviewer: overrides.reviewer ?? null,\r\n    artifacts: overrides.artifacts,\r\n    allowedExecutables: overrides.allowedExecutables ?? new Set(),\r\n    capabilities: {\r\n      networkSandbox: { active: false, receipt: null },\r\n      mutation: false,\r\n      browser: false,\r\n      signing: false,\r\n      reviewing: false,\r\n      nativeSurface: false,\r\n      mobileDevice: false,\r\n      externalEvidence: false,\r\n      ...(overrides.capabilities ?? {}),\r\n    },\r\n  };\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### SNIP-04 — artifact record\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\r\n  \"kind\": \"nemesis-artifact-record\",\r\n  \"path\": \"provider-results/copy.json\",\r\n  \"digest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"bytes\": 0,\r\n  \"producer\": \"copy.core\",\r\n  \"producerVersion\": \"1.0.0\",\r\n  \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\r\n  \"binding\": {},\r\n  \"denominatorDigest\": \"sha256:...\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWrite to a temporary file, flush, atomically replace, read the final bytes, compute the digest, and then add the record to `run-manifest.json`.\r\n\r\n### SNIP-05 — canonical binding\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"planDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"repositoryRevision\": \"...\",\r\n  \"dirtyOverlayDigest\": \"sha256:... or null\",\r\n  \"cortexProvider\": \"@orthic-labs/cortex\",\r\n  \"cortexVersion\": \"...\",\r\n  \"cortexGenerationId\": \"...\",\r\n  \"cortexManifestDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"familyRegistryDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"providerRegistryDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"skillRegistryDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"productTargetRegistryDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"componentRegistryDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"stackCatalogueDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"controlSourceCatalogueDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"controlPackRegistryDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"scenarioRegistryDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"releaseContractDigest\": \"sha256:... or null\",\r\n  \"externalEvidenceManifestDigest\": \"sha256:... or null\",\r\n  \"configDigest\": \"sha256:...\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nEvery consumer calls `assertArtifactBinding(artifact, expectedBinding, label)` before using an upstream artifact.\r\n\r\n### SNIP-06 — skill bundle manifest\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\r\n  \"kind\": \"nemesis-skill-bundle\",\r\n  \"id\": \"designer\",\r\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\r\n  \"rootUri\": \"nemesis-skill://designer/\",\r\n  \"entry\": \"SKILL.md\",\r\n  \"files\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"uri\": \"nemesis-skill://designer/SKILL.md\",\r\n      \"path\": \"skills/designer/SKILL.md\",\r\n      \"sourceDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n      \"outputDigest\": \"sha256:...\"\r\n    }\r\n  ],\r\n  \"transformations\": [\r\n    \"internal-uri-rewrite\",\r\n    \"public-product-neutralization\",\r\n    \"audit-profile-extraction\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"provenance\": {\r\n    \"source\": \"user-supplied\",\r\n    \"license\": \"owner-supplied\",\r\n    \"importedAt\": \"...\"\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### SNIP-07 — content item\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\r\n  \"kind\": \"nemesis-content-item\",\r\n  \"id\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"text\": \"Delete account\",\r\n  \"textDigest\": \"sha256:...\",\r\n  \"source\": {\r\n    \"file\": \"src/settings/DeleteAccount.tsx\",\r\n    \"start\": { \"line\": 44, \"column\": 12 },\r\n    \"end\": { \"line\": 44, \"column\": 28 }\r\n  },\r\n  \"surface\": {\r\n    \"type\": \"button\",\r\n    \"component\": \"DeleteAccount\",\r\n    \"route\": \"/sett